capability regex/compile  a pattern compiled once, failing with where and why
  heroes  regex.compile
  go      regexp.Compile, regexp.MustCompile
  rust    -
  python  re.compile, re.error, re.PatternError (msg, pattern, pos, lineno, colno)
  note    the failure is `invalid` and its message names the byte of the pattern and the reason; `MustCompile` is `.must()`; a pattern is one line, so Python's line and column are the byte

capability regex/compiled  a compiled pattern as a value a program holds and passes
  heroes  regex.Regex
  go      regexp.Regexp
  rust    -
  python  re.Pattern
  note    Rust has no regular expression in its standard library, so every `rust` line of this ledger is `-`

capability regex/pattern-text  the text a pattern was compiled from
  heroes  regex.Regex
  go      Regexp.String
  rust    -
  python  Pattern.pattern
  note    the record's field `pattern`, the text as written

capability regex/linear-time  a search whose time is linear in the text, whatever the pattern
  heroes  regex.search
  go      regexp (the package's guarantee)
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    every search runs the automaton with every state at once; measured 2026-11-16, `(a+)+$` against thirty `a`s and a `b` took 0.00 s of user time and against a million 0.30 s, where Python took 0.07, 0.25, 0.99 and 3.91 s for 20, 22, 24 and 26

capability regex/bounded-size  a pattern that cannot ask for an automaton too large to build
  heroes  regex.compile
  go      regexp.Compile (a count above 1000 is refused, and a pattern that grows too large)
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    fails `limit` past `MAX_REPEAT` (1000, Go's bound), `MAX_STATES` (100000), `MAX_GROUPS` (200) and `MAX_NESTING` (1000, Go's); Go refuses `(a{1000}){1000}` too

capability regex/flags  case ignored, `.` reading a line feed, `^` and `$` at every line
  heroes  regex.compile
  go      regexp.Compile (`(?i)`, `(?s)`, `(?m)` in the pattern)
  rust    -
  python  re.I, re.IGNORECASE, re.S, re.DOTALL, re.M, re.MULTILINE, re.RegexFlag, re.NOFLAG, Pattern.flags
  note    `(?i)` stands anywhere and holds to the end of its group, Go's rule; Python 3.15 refuses a flag that does not open the pattern

capability regex/flag-ascii  `\d`, `\w`, `\s` and `\b` reading ASCII alone
  heroes  regex.compile
  go      regexp.Compile (always so)
  rust    -
  python  re.A, re.ASCII
  note    `\d`, `\w`, `\s` and `\b` are always ASCII, Go's rule; `\s` holds the vertical tab, Python's ASCII rule, where Go's does not; Unicode's classes are asked by name, `\p{Nd}`

capability regex/unicode-classes  a class of characters named by Unicode's category or script
  heroes  regex.compile
  go      regexp.Compile (`\pL`, `\p{Lu}`, `\PL`, `\P{Lu}`)
  rust    -
  python  re.U, re.UNICODE
  note    the general categories, one letter or two, and their negations; a script is the block `regex/unicode-scripts`

capability regex/unicode-scripts  a class of characters named by a script
  heroes  pending
  go      regexp.Compile (`\p{Greek}`, `\P{Greek}`)
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    waits for `unicode` to answer a character's script, its own block `class/script` being pending; `\p{Greek}` fails `invalid` until then

capability regex/ascii-classes  a class of ASCII named inside brackets
  heroes  regex.compile
  go      regexp.Compile (`[[:alpha:]]`, `[[:^digit:]]` and the other twelve)
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `[:alpha:]` outside brackets is refused with the spelling `[[:alpha:]]`, which Go and Python read as a class of the characters `:alph`

capability regex/flag-locale  classes and case read from the process's locale
  heroes  refused: what a pattern matches does not change with the process's locale
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  re.L, re.LOCALE

capability regex/flag-verbose  a pattern written over several lines, with comments
  heroes  refused: a pattern is one line, and what a comment would say about it is written above it
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  re.X, re.VERBOSE, `(?#...)`

capability regex/flag-ungreedy  every repetition lazy unless it says otherwise
  heroes  refused: one way: a repetition says it is lazy with its own `?`
  go      regexp.Compile (`(?U)`)
  rust    -
  python  -

capability regex/longest  the longest match at the leftmost place, in place of the one Perl prefers
  heroes  refused: one rule of preference for every search, Perl's, which Go's default and Python share; Go's own documentation calls the POSIX rule computationally prohibitive and not even well-defined
  go      regexp.CompilePOSIX, regexp.MustCompilePOSIX, Regexp.Longest
  rust    -
  python  -

capability regex/back-references  a group's text asked again later in the pattern
  heroes  refused: not matchable in linear time
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  re.compile (`\1`, `(?P=name)`, `(?(1)yes|no)`)

capability regex/look-around  what stands before or after asked without being matched
  heroes  refused: not matchable in linear time
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  re.compile (`(?=...)`, `(?!...)`, `(?<=...)`, `(?<!...)`)

capability regex/possessive  a repetition or a group that gives nothing back
  heroes  refused: an automaton never gives anything back, so a repetition that gives nothing back would only be a second rule of preference beside Perl's
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  re.compile (`*+`, `++`, `?+`, `{n,m}+`, `(?>...)`)

capability regex/code-escapes  a character written by its code
  heroes  regex.compile
  go      regexp.Compile (`\x7F`, `\x{10FFFF}`)
  rust    -
  python  re.compile (`\xhh`, `\uhhhh`, `\Uhhhhhhhh`)
  note    `\x41` and `\x{1f600}`; Python's `\u0041` is refused, naming `\x{...}`

capability regex/octal-escapes  a character written by its code in octal
  heroes  refused: one way: a code is written in hexadecimal, `\x{...}`; `\12` would read as octal beside the back-reference `\1`
  go      regexp.Compile (`\123`)
  rust    -
  python  re.compile (`\0`, `\012`)

capability regex/name-escapes  a character written by its Unicode name
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  re.compile (`\N{NAME}`)
  note    waits for a table of Unicode's character names, which `unicode` does not hold

capability regex/literal-run  a run of text inside a pattern taken as it is
  heroes  refused: one way: `regex.escape` quotes a text
  go      regexp.Compile (`\Q...\E`)
  rust    -
  python  -

capability regex/escape  a text quoted so that, as a pattern, it matches itself
  heroes  regex.escape
  go      regexp.QuoteMeta
  rust    -
  python  re.escape
  note    escapes `\ . + * ? ( ) | [ ] { } ^ $ -`: Go's set with `-`, so a quoted text also stands inside a class; Python also escapes space, `#`, `&` and `~`

capability regex/contains  whether a text holds a match
  heroes  regex.contains
  go      regexp.MatchString, regexp.Match, Regexp.MatchString, Regexp.Match
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Python asks `search` and compares with `None`; Go's two functions of the package compile the pattern at each call, which here is `compile`, once

capability regex/search  the first match, and where it is
